00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.380
When you look at the
overall cloud evolution,

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.770
we've gone through these
phases every 10 years.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:11.009
And we are currently somewhere
between the red and blue dot,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.680
in terms of evolution taking
place today that's really

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.070
been driven by transformation
that containers have really

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.110
brought to the market.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.880
For a long time, the
only path forward

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:24.060
was really from an
infrastructure as a service

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.850
perspective, a lift
and shift to the cloud,

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.800
and then modernized
in the cloud.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.540
And we've really
been looking at ways,

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.110
in terms of bringing all the
various goodness of the cloud,

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.840
to you wherever you're running
your application so that you

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.060
can modernize where
you are and move

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:40.980
to the cloud at your own pace.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.840
So this trend that
we're seeing right now

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.860
around cloud-native
services is really something

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.710
that the market
is picking up on.

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:52.650
And I have to say, in my entire
career in the IT industry,

00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.660
I haven't seen
this much traction

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.890
as I'm seeing right now.

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.430
This whole notion of being
able to build services that

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.550
can literally run anywhere
and being able to manage them

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.540
from a central location, I
think, is key to this trend

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.750
that we're seeing right now
around cloud native services.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:14.220
And then Hybrid has really
become an enterprise reality.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:17.220
I'm sure a lot of
you have applications

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:22.730
that probably fall into any
one of these categories here.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:26.130
For a very long
time, the only path

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.400
was really to go from
on-prem to public cloud.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.110
And that's a great path.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.530
But as Diane alluded to
in the keynote, only 10%

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.990
of applications
today have really

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.890
migrated to the public cloud.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.410
There's a reason for that.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.390
Running and migrating
applications

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.740
from on-prem to the
public cloud is hard.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.230
Many organizations are
looking at figuring out

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.920
how to take a monolith, break
it up into microservices,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.050
figure out where to run
these microservices--

00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:56.720
do you run them on-prem?

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.510
Do you run them in the cloud?

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.200
How do you connect
these services together?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.540
Some of you probably
have portfolios

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:05.580
of thousands of applications.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.220
Each application has
different characteristics

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.530
and data privacy requirements.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.000
Some have to stay on-prem.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.680
Some can easily
move to the cloud.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.230
How do you figure all that out?

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.260
But the key here is, how do
you manage that portfolio

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.510
of application in such a
way where you're doing it

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.860
consistently without having
to use this tool for on-prem,

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.456
this tool for cloud, this
tool for microservices,

00:04:29.456 --> 00:04:31.080
a different tool for
policy management,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.810
a different tool for security?

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.520
Those are really hard problems.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.070
Our goal here really is
to bridge these two worlds

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.390
together.

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:48.540
So on one hand, you have
the modernization evolution

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.510
that's happening from
an on-prem standpoint.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.900
You have administrators
and architects

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.290
looking at taking workloads that
can't easily move to the cloud

00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.510
and modernizing them.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:02.550
And the path that we're seeing
here with a lot of customers

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.610
is they're taking
tools like Kubernetes

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:09.440
and figuring out how to run
them in on-prem environments

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.300
so that they can take
existing applications

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:15.780
and containerize them and
modernize in place so that they

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.840
can eventually move to
the public cloud at a pace

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.520
that they're comfortable with.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.000
At the same time, there's
this very fast move

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.800
that's happening
where developers

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.440
are going off and containerizing
their applications

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.990
and running them in the cloud.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:34.740
So as an organization,
you have to be

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.260
able to manage all these
various requirements that

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.800
are coming from your
developers, from your IT admins,

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.270
from your architects.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.730
How do you do that?

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:45.900
What we're going to talk
about today is really

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.340
bridging together all these
various components, where

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:55.560
a developer can go from
source to image to container

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.740
to deployment environment,
and then a admin can

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.070
come along and specify
policy associated with how

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:04.170
these applications
run together--

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.510
who can talk to
what, who has access

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.640
to various components
within the architecture?

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.120
So all these various
roles would work together,

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:15.270
in terms of providing that
end-to-end state where you're

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.230
getting application agility,
you're getting faster time

00:06:19.230 --> 00:06:21.720
to market, and at
the same time, IT

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.040
has control over
the environment.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.880
And you're abstracting away
the underlying complexities--

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:29.230
the infrastructure--
as much as possible.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:31.000
This is our goal.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.990
So what if you can just
focus on innovating,

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:41.850
focus on writing code, and
then have the platform solve

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.360
all the gnarly problems
associated with connectivity,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.960
associated with
policy enforcement,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.780
and make it in such a way
that it's all API-driven

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.030
and you get the
best of both worlds?

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:55.880
What if you can do that?

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.730
So at Google, our open
hybrid cloud strategy

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.970
has really been focused
on three core tenants.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.260
The first is Kubernetes.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.330
Kubernetes has been
growing like crazy.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:13.740
Since its introduction into
the open source world--

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:15.690
been four years now--

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.300
Kubernetes has seen
dramatic growth

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.790
and is now the de
facto orchestrator

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:23.520
for containers on the market.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.350
And what Kubernetes
allows you to do

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:29.460
is build services and run
services and deploy services

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:32.490
very quickly in a
continuous fashion.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:36.330
It makes it more predictable to
make changes to applications.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.450
And it gives you the ability
now to run these services

00:07:39.450 --> 00:07:41.160
in environments that
are heterogeneous

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.910
in nature, whether it's on one
cloud, on different clouds,

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:48.360
on-premise, without having
to rewrite your code.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.730
That's the beauty of Kubernetes.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.880
Then there's another component
called Open Service Broker.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:57.030
Open Service Broker is really a
standard around consuming cloud

00:07:57.030 --> 00:08:00.090
services where you can consume
these services pretty much

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.360
from anywhere.

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.760
And this is also an
open source project.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:05.280
And then finally, Istio.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.520
And I'm sure a lot of you
been hearing this week a lot

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:10.420
of information about Istio.

00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:12.390
Istio is another
open source project

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.030
that was developed in
conjunction between Google,

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:19.680
Lyft, and IBM, where it solves
the challenges associated

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.420
with managing all these various
services that you've created

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:25.920
in heterogeneous environments.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:28.110
How do you connect
these services together?

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:30.870
How do you ensure that
connectivity is secure

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:34.020
and get observability around
what these services are doing?

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.000
So if you were in the
keynote, you probably

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.830
saw a really cool demo around
how Istio can get metrics,

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:43.590
and then Stackdriver exposed
all dependency graphs,

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.800
in terms of how your services
are communicating together.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.210
And Rohit is going to go really
deep into Istio in our talk

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.870
and give you really a
high level around what it

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:55.870
is, how it works.

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:57.720
And our demo will
actually be using Istio.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.060
So the Cisco hybrid
platform for Google Cloud

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:07.630
is really about one very
important key tenet--

00:09:07.630 --> 00:09:12.070
is the ability to write
once, deploy anywhere.

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.910
It's about being able to
build your application,

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:18.700
take your containers,
run them on Kubernetes,

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.950
and be able to run
those containers,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:24.490
whether it's on Google
Kubernetes Engine or on-premise

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.740
on Cisco Container Platform.

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:31.810
The whole idea is, it's really
about agility and control.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:33.970
You shouldn't have to
rewrite your application

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.700
when you move it from
environment to environment.

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.220
And this is the goal that we're
targeting together, in terms

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.040
of building this offering.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:44.950
So why are we doing this?

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:51.550
The key here is, "hybrid" has
really become an overused term.

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:53.930
A lot of times when
people think about hybrid,

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.730
they think about a
single application that

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:00.280
spans multiple environments.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.590
As a matter of fact,
that's a small portion

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:05.950
of the hybrid use cases
that exist out there today.

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:10.570
Many cases, hybrid is about
managing different applications

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:13.990
in different environments
in a consistent fashion.

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:16.180
So as people adopt
a hybrid strategy,

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.670
they run into big challenges.

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.510
First is connectivity.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:22.990
How do you connect
from on-premise

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:25.840
to the public cloud
in a secure fashion,

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:28.280
in a fashion where you
have control around it,

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.060
and you can enforce policy
around who has access to what?

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:37.200
How do you spin up these
Kubernetes environments

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.550
anywhere and do it
in such a way that's

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:41.650
consistent with the
experience that you

00:10:41.650 --> 00:10:44.180
have running in the cloud?

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:47.020
How do you manage these
Kubernetes environments,

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:48.880
again, consistently,
where you're

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:52.510
writing your application once
and deploying them anywhere?

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:55.750
And then support-- who's
going to support this?

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.590
You want a single support
arm that will support

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.900
your entire hybrid deployment.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.520
That's the key.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:06.070
And this is what Cisco
brings to the table

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.290
in our joint hybrid offering.

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:11.230
These are very hard
problems to solve.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:13.480
And Google and Cisco
partner together

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:15.640
to bring really the
best of both worlds,

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:18.730
in terms of what both
companies bring to the table.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:22.210
From a Google standpoint,
we bring Kubernetes.

00:11:22.210 --> 00:11:24.400
We bring a lot of
the innovations

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:27.070
that we put forward, from
an open source standpoint,

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:29.740
around Kubernetes and Istio
and the work that we've

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.350
done around Google
Kubernetes Engine.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.500
We also bring a set
of services that we've

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.590
developed around application
connectivity and all

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:40.020
these various components.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.600
So together, we're bringing the
best of what both companies do

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:45.130
and offering it in
such a way where

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:49.720
it's turnkey and consumable
and supported consistently

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.730
across your hybrid environment.

00:11:51.730 --> 00:11:54.560
And that's our goal.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:57.220
So some of the use cases
that we're enabling--

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:00.860
and we'll be talking
in greater detail,

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:02.480
especially around
the third use case.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.660
But the first few case is, how
do I connect from public cloud

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:11.140
to a system that's
sitting on-premise that

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:13.540
can't move to the public cloud?

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:19.670
Think of a legacy system or
some non-REST-based interface

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.100
that isn't modernized.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:22.610
How do I do that?

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:24.860
This is the first use
case that we've enabled.

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:27.580
The second is
really about, how do

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:32.200
I consume cloud services from
on-prem, from Kubernetes,

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:35.410
in such a way that's consistent
across my environment?

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:37.640
Maybe I'd like to consume
from Google Cloud.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.790
Maybe I want to consume
from other clouds.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:43.150
But I want to consume
these services using

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:45.070
a consistent interface.

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:48.550
And then the third use case is
really around hybrid CI/CD--

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:51.010
Continuous Integration
Development--

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:54.550
where I'd like to be able to
write my application, perhaps,

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:57.310
for the public cloud,
but then move it

00:12:57.310 --> 00:12:59.830
around different
environments without having

00:12:59.830 --> 00:13:01.510
to rewrite my application.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.690
Maybe I want to do
canary in public cloud,

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.630
and then trickle requests,
and then eventually push it

00:13:07.630 --> 00:13:10.060
into production, or
blue-green tests and things

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:12.190
of that nature--
blue-green deployments.

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:13.750
That's really
about the third use

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:16.390
case-- the CI/CD deployments.

00:13:16.390 --> 00:13:20.380
So for the first
use case, the idea

00:13:20.380 --> 00:13:24.040
here is we want to
enable our customers

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:27.040
to be able to innovate
and build applications

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.490
in the public cloud.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:30.250
But then these
applications should easily

00:13:30.250 --> 00:13:33.340
be able to connect with systems
that are sitting in your data

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:34.180
centers.

00:13:34.180 --> 00:13:38.230
And we do that by
leveraging Google Apigee.

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:41.440
And Google Apigee
is an API gateway

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:47.460
that really takes interfaces
that are non-modern,

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.220
non-REST interfaces, and it's a
layer that sits in front of it

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:53.590
and makes these
interfaces consumable

00:13:53.590 --> 00:13:54.910
by modern applications.

00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:57.160
So think of a use
case where you've

00:13:57.160 --> 00:14:02.020
got a system that's storing
customer data that can't

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:03.350
move into the public cloud.

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:05.140
Maybe you're in a
regulated industry

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.540
where the data can't
move country boundaries

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.020
or things of that nature.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.290
And then you want to
build a mobile app that

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:14.260
needs to really scale for
performance standpoint.

00:14:14.260 --> 00:14:17.120
And you build that mobile
app in the public cloud.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.930
Now that mobile app can handle
all the various requests that

00:14:19.930 --> 00:14:22.090
are coming in, from a
front-end standpoint,

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.150
and scale efficiently,
but then can communicate

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.500
with these legacy systems
that are sitting in your data

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:30.190
center via the Apigee gateway.

00:14:30.190 --> 00:14:31.750
That's the first use case.

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:35.140
And the key here is, the work
that we're doing with Cisco

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.470
is really about
pulling together--

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:43.630
they're called approved
reference architectures

00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:48.610
that give you these capabilities
in a deployment model

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:50.590
so that you're not
having to install

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:52.780
all the various
components separately.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:56.650
You want connectivity--
there's a Cisco CVD that

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:58.810
basically addresses
this use case

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:03.600
and gives you this particular
architectural pattern.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:07.230
The second use case is
about consuming Google Cloud

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:07.800
Services.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:10.110
So in Google Cloud,
we have this service

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.090
called Open Service Broker.

00:15:12.090 --> 00:15:14.400
And what Open
Service Broker does

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.880
is it gives you the ability
to consume Google Cloud

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:22.170
Services from outside of Google
Cloud in a consistent fashion

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:25.860
so that you don't have to worry
about the semantics associated

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.280
with things like
service accounts,

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:32.970
having to figure out how
to translate information

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.240
from on-prem to the
public cloud service.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.580
Open Service Broker
basically leverages

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.210
a component within Kubernetes
called Service Catalog, which

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:45.420
initiates a session
with Open Service Broker

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.840
and then passes the tokens
that gives you the ability

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.300
now to communicate
with that cloud service

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.910
and consume it from
outside of Google Cloud.

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.810
So it abstracts away the
complexities around security,

00:15:57.810 --> 00:16:00.630
around the various
semantics associated

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.870
with that consumption pattern.

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.520
And then the third
use case is really

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.420
about a hybrid CI/CD pattern.

00:16:09.420 --> 00:16:11.640
So the idea here
is, suppose I have

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:15.390
a containerized application
running in production.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:17.610
I've got a new
version coming out.

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:20.660
I can deploy that new
version in Google Cloud,

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:25.840
run some canary testing,
and then be able to-- once

00:16:25.840 --> 00:16:28.350
I'm positive, from
a policy standpoint,

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.280
that things are
working properly--

00:16:30.280 --> 00:16:33.480
I can now have that
trickle into production

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:36.330
and have a new version
that's running,

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:39.820
and no impact on
my user experience.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:41.370
And this is what
Rohit is actually

00:16:41.370 --> 00:16:44.220
going to do a demo
later on in our session

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.810
and show you how that all works.

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:50.690
And before I hand it over to
Rohit to talk about the Cisco

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.910
components, let me
just spend a bit

00:16:52.910 --> 00:16:55.910
of time talking about
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.720
So I'm sure some of you
have been in sessions

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:04.280
and watched the Kubernetes
Engine pitch and so forth.

00:17:04.280 --> 00:17:07.250
But I want to highlight
just a few things.

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:10.280
So Google Kubernetes Engine
has been a GA product now

00:17:10.280 --> 00:17:12.710
for three years.

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:15.260
It is a managed
Kubernetes service that

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:17.630
runs on Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:20.300
It is 100% upstream Kubernetes.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:23.516
This is not two versions behind.

00:17:23.516 --> 00:17:25.390
Whenever there's a new
version of Kubernetes,

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:29.030
which is every three months,
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:33.170
supports that new version
within a couple of weeks.

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:36.140
Within a Kubernetes cluster,
there's usually a master,

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:38.000
and there is worker nodes.

00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:41.180
With Kubernetes Engine,
Google manages the master.

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.370
We give you the ability
to auto-upgrade.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:47.120
Any time there's a new
version, we'll notify you.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:50.900
We will also auto-upgrade
your nodes, again,

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:51.860
through notification.

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:54.590
It's really up to you
to tell us how you want

00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:56.480
those upgrades to take place.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:59.136
We have an SRE team sitting
at Google that manages

00:17:59.136 --> 00:18:00.260
the health of that cluster.

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:02.930
Anything happens, we fix it.

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:06.740
We ensure your applications
are running, are healthy.

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:09.720
It is integrated with the
underlying Google Cloud

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:10.490
auto-scaler.

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:13.340
So you're not only getting
container auto-scaling,

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:16.160
but you're getting
underlying node auto-scaling.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:19.610
So you can define thresholds
and integrate with Stackdriver

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.320
for logging and monitoring.

00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:23.430
Its built on top of
Google's network,

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:25.089
so it's a global network.

00:18:25.089 --> 00:18:27.380
You can have clusters running
in different regions that

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:29.420
can talk to each other.

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:32.330
And from a compliance
standpoint,

00:18:32.330 --> 00:18:36.530
it is certified across all the
various regulatory compliance--

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:39.360
HIPAA, PCI, and so forth.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.230
And again, I just want
to stress that this

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:44.850
is upstream Kubernetes
and 100% compatible,

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:48.500
which means that when you
build applications on GKE,

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:50.390
those applications
can easily run

00:18:50.390 --> 00:18:54.630
on Kubernetes' implementations
outside Google Cloud as well.

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:56.540
So with that said, I'll
hand it over to Rohit

00:18:56.540 --> 00:19:00.014
to talk about Cisco
Container Platform.

00:19:00.014 --> 00:19:01.305
ROHIT AGARWALLA: Thanks, Allan.

00:19:01.305 --> 00:19:06.930
So as Allan mentioned,
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:19:06.930 --> 00:19:09.480
is kind of a great
place for you to get

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.010
a cloud-managed
Kubernetes environment.

00:19:12.010 --> 00:19:14.610
But from Cisco's perspective,
and overall as the partnership

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.520
with Google, what we have
been hearing from customers

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.760
is they also want to start on
the on-prem side, as described

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:23.364
in the third use case, where
you want to maybe start

00:19:23.364 --> 00:19:24.780
in the public
cloud, but also want

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.222
to deploy it on-prem as
you move into production

00:19:27.222 --> 00:19:29.430
or you have multiple edge
locations on which you want

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:33.020
to deploy your Kubernetes apps.

00:19:33.020 --> 00:19:34.710
So we wanted to
essentially make sure

00:19:34.710 --> 00:19:36.990
that we give you a
certified Kubernetes

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.264
platform available
on the on-prem that

00:19:39.264 --> 00:19:40.680
integrates with
the infrastructure

00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.600
that you have on
the on-prem side.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.190
And we talk about
the infrastructure,

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:45.780
because our customers--

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:48.430
as our IT administrators,
the CIOs--

00:19:48.430 --> 00:19:51.450
they have invested in
their infrastructure

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:53.100
over multiple years.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:55.230
And as the transition
to the cloud happens,

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.780
they want to make sure that
that investment continues

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.970
to pay off as the transition
the cloud is happening.

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:05.040
So giving you a
production-ready, turnkey

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.500
container platform on
the on-premise side

00:20:07.500 --> 00:20:10.290
helps to actually get benefit
out of your infrastructure

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:11.320
investments as well.

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:13.150
And as we go through
the presentation,

00:20:13.150 --> 00:20:15.870
you will see how this integrates
with Cisco's infrastructure

00:20:15.870 --> 00:20:17.310
specifically.

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:20.130
So as you can see on the
first and foremost point,

00:20:20.130 --> 00:20:22.990
this is a fully 100%
upstream Kubernetes.

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:24.540
In fact, this is
certified platform.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:28.729
We ran a bunch of CNCF tests
to make sure it's compatible.

00:20:28.729 --> 00:20:31.020
It is hybrid cloud optimized
from a Google perspective.

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:33.460
So over the last
year, as we announced

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:36.780
the partnership back in October,
Cisco's Kubernetes engineering

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:39.300
team has been working very
closely with Google Kubernetes

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:42.570
Engine team to ensure some of
the configs and other things

00:20:42.570 --> 00:20:45.060
that are needed on a
consistent environment

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:46.830
of the on-prem side,
we support that

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.530
as part of the deployment-- so
things like operating system

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.350
where we start with Ubuntu
in a very similar way in both

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:53.820
these environments.

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:56.670
Again, one of the things that
was mentioned earlier today

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:59.100
in the keynote by
[? AL ?] and Kip Compton

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:01.290
was around how Cisco
Container Platform

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:04.350
is the first and only
GKE-certified platform

00:21:04.350 --> 00:21:08.010
and consistent with GKE
running on the on-prem side.

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:09.810
Third, from an
integration perspective,

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:11.940
we extend not just beyond
networking and storage,

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:15.180
but also from a cloud management
and analytics perspective,

00:21:15.180 --> 00:21:16.770
we have all of
the Cisco products

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:19.320
that integrate to give you
a comprehensive multi-cloud

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:21.510
capability around the
Kubernetes environment

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:23.727
that's running on
the on-prem side.

00:21:23.727 --> 00:21:25.560
And then finally, from
a flexible deployment

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.660
perspective, we
understand on the on-prem,

00:21:27.660 --> 00:21:30.630
you have investments into
virtual machines where you're

00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:32.640
deploying your
existing workloads

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.160
either on virtual machines
or bare-metal servers.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:36.690
So we are giving
you the flexibility

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:38.520
to first spin up these
Kubernetes clusters

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:39.799
within virtual machines.

00:21:39.799 --> 00:21:41.340
But in the future,
we'll also support

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.410
bare-metal infrastructure
where we'll give you

00:21:43.410 --> 00:21:45.690
all the automation that's
needed under the hood

00:21:45.690 --> 00:21:47.874
to bring up a full
infrastructure that

00:21:47.874 --> 00:21:50.290
is ready for deploying all of
these Kubernetes [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:51.570
on top of that.

00:21:51.570 --> 00:21:54.360
In addition to this,
again, Cisco's networking

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:57.120
infrastructure, from
an SDN perspective,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:58.750
we integrate with that as well.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:02.471
And we'll get into those
details just in a minute.

00:22:02.471 --> 00:22:03.970
So the first
integration that I want

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:06.400
to talk about, from an
infrastructure perspective,

00:22:06.400 --> 00:22:08.730
is with HyperFlex platform.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:11.219
Our HyperFlex platform
is essentially

00:22:11.219 --> 00:22:12.760
a hyper-converged
infrastructure that

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:17.150
combines compute and storage
within an on-prem environment.

00:22:17.150 --> 00:22:21.134
And again, I'll be providing
you with a built-in iS platform.

00:22:21.134 --> 00:22:23.800
So for our virtual machines, you
are getting the data management

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:27.070
capabilities in terms of
replication, as well as HA,

00:22:27.070 --> 00:22:28.480
on the on-prem side.

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:31.180
But with the Kubernetes and
the Cisco Container Platform

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:34.000
integration, we wanted
to extend that support

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.100
to pods and Kubernetes
environment as well.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.590
And so the way we
have done that is

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:41.680
we have developed a FlexVolume
driver that essentially gives

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.680
you the persistent volume
support for pods or containers

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:47.274
that are running within
a Kubernetes environment.

00:22:47.274 --> 00:22:48.690
And the good part
is that this is,

00:22:48.690 --> 00:22:52.150
as a turnkey configuration,
as an administrator

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:54.940
on the on-prem side, deploys
a Kubernetes cluster that

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:56.920
can pick and choose
the storage driver

00:22:56.920 --> 00:22:59.660
that they want to enable
for the persistent volume.

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:02.500
So in case of pods move
from one node to the other

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.360
or your virtual machines move
from one node to the other,

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.510
your persistent volume
continues to be there,

00:23:07.510 --> 00:23:10.060
because we chunk the data as
we write them into the disk,

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:13.690
using the software that
is running from a storage

00:23:13.690 --> 00:23:15.910
perspective to chunk
across the multiple nodes,

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:17.560
as well as create
the replication

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.000
in case of node failures.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.190
So all of those data
management capabilities

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:23.454
that you have with virtual
machines on HyperFlex,

00:23:23.454 --> 00:23:25.120
you get with containers
that are running

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:29.150
within Kubernetes as part of
the Cisco Container Platform.

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:31.970
From a networking perspective,
Cisco's Application Centric

00:23:31.970 --> 00:23:32.660
Infrastructure--

00:23:32.660 --> 00:23:35.150
ACI-- that's our SDN controller.

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:38.810
And it essentially provides
you with network connection

00:23:38.810 --> 00:23:41.900
as part of the SDN fabric, as
well as policy implementation,

00:23:41.900 --> 00:23:44.390
using our Application Policy
Infrastructure Controller

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:45.650
called APIC.

00:23:45.650 --> 00:23:48.980
And what we have done here is
we have created a Kubernetes CNI

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:51.410
driver, or Container
Networking Interface driver,

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:53.960
that essentially gives you
both Kubernetes network

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:56.180
connectivity, as well as
Kubernetes network policy

00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:58.790
implementation within
the ACI fabric.

00:23:58.790 --> 00:24:00.830
So imagine having an
on-prem environment

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:02.844
where the ACI is
in fabric, where

00:24:02.844 --> 00:24:04.260
you have bare-metal
infrastructure

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:06.950
of virtual machines, as well
as now containers running

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:08.570
with Cisco Container Platform.

00:24:08.570 --> 00:24:10.580
You can essentially
have the same fabric

00:24:10.580 --> 00:24:13.490
within your on-prem environment
to give connectivity and apply

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:15.700
policies in a very
consistent way.

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.290
And we scale in a way so
that the fabric is actually

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:20.330
taking care of all the
network connectivity,

00:24:20.330 --> 00:24:23.330
as well as implementing quality,
rather than doing everything

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:27.572
on the host and overloading our
hosts with those constructs.

00:24:27.572 --> 00:24:29.030
The third integration
piece that we

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:31.700
have done as part of the
partnership and integration

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:34.160
is from a multi-cloud
application management

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:35.360
perspective.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:38.360
Cisco Cloud Center essentially
supported deployment

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:40.430
of virtual machines,
and also talking

00:24:40.430 --> 00:24:43.400
to other cloud services, and
defining a governance policy

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:46.070
in terms of what administrators
and what developers

00:24:46.070 --> 00:24:49.490
can access to and what ports,
for example, can be opened up.

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:51.920
But we've also added the
support for Kubernetes.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:55.460
So essentially, you can
define a YAML definition

00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:58.310
or give a YAML definition
in Cloud Center and point

00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:01.490
to a Kubernetes API, whether
that's running on top of CCP

00:25:01.490 --> 00:25:04.454
or running within GKE, and have
a very consistent experience

00:25:04.454 --> 00:25:06.620
in terms of deploying this
across the multiple cloud

00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:07.700
environments.

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:09.799
Again, with the Cloud
Center UI, you also

00:25:09.799 --> 00:25:11.840
get the capabilities to
embed some of the agents,

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:14.630
such as [INAUDIBLE] agent,
which we recently announced

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:16.140
Kubernetes support as well.

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:18.080
And so it gives you
a turnkey experience

00:25:18.080 --> 00:25:19.940
of not just deploying
the application,

00:25:19.940 --> 00:25:22.520
but also embedding agents
from an application monitoring

00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:25.009
and visibility
perspective as well.

00:25:25.009 --> 00:25:27.050
Now keep in mind, all of
these three integrations

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:28.674
I have talked about
from a use case

00:25:28.674 --> 00:25:30.590
three perspective, which
is, we are giving you

00:25:30.590 --> 00:25:34.459
a very consistent environment
across GKE, as well as on-prem.

00:25:34.459 --> 00:25:36.500
And we've talked about
the HyperFlex integration,

00:25:36.500 --> 00:25:37.600
which gives us storage.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:40.100
We've talked about the ACI
integration, which gives us

00:25:40.100 --> 00:25:41.820
the networking,
and Cloud Center,

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:45.050
which gives us the application
deployment construct.

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:47.300
The last piece that I want
to talk about this use case

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:49.520
and then dive into
the demo is Istio.

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:53.114
And I'm pretty sure you've heard
about Istio all over this week.

00:25:53.114 --> 00:25:55.280
And so Istio is, again,
like an open-source project,

00:25:55.280 --> 00:25:57.650
like Allan mentioned,
that was open source

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:01.340
by Google, IBM, and Lyft
about a year or so back.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:03.980
And Cisco's got involved
into this open source project

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:04.970
as well.

00:26:04.970 --> 00:26:07.700
And you'll see in the demo how
we are leveraging in use case

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:08.690
three, Istio--

00:26:08.690 --> 00:26:12.110
how it plays a complete role
in defining your CI/CD platform

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:14.420
and deployments for
canary and A/B/n testing.

00:26:14.420 --> 00:26:16.820
So very quick high level
overview of Istio--

00:26:16.820 --> 00:26:19.090
first and foremost, it
gives you service discovery,

00:26:19.090 --> 00:26:22.190
and not just for containers,
but also for other workloads.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:25.220
Essentially by running that
sidecar proxy called Envoy,

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.050
which is what Istio
is supporting,

00:26:27.050 --> 00:26:28.820
you can help to
dynamically discover

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:30.710
your services
across a multi-cloud

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:32.630
or a hybrid environment.

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:35.000
Then in terms of having
that type of proxy,

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:37.710
you can now offload a lot
of the functionality that

00:26:37.710 --> 00:26:39.440
is very common to
your applications

00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:40.940
into your sidecar proxy.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:43.280
And this could be things like
circuit breaking, failure

00:26:43.280 --> 00:26:45.420
recovery, load balancing.

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:48.080
So things that have been
traditionally done specifically

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:50.060
in the application
or by appliances

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:53.030
in a central location, now you
can have a distributed model

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:55.400
of implementing all of these
logic within that sidecar

00:26:55.400 --> 00:26:56.270
proxy.

00:26:56.270 --> 00:26:57.890
But Istio gives you
that control plane

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:00.500
to define how those
things should behave.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:03.980
Now since that sidecar proxy
is in the path of deployment

00:27:03.980 --> 00:27:07.520
or your traffic, you can now
also get all the [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:10.010
observability between
your services.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:11.660
So that gives you
information about,

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:14.480
what is the communication graph
between different services?

00:27:14.480 --> 00:27:15.710
What is the latency?

00:27:15.710 --> 00:27:18.980
So you can get all of
those, in terms of a GKE

00:27:18.980 --> 00:27:21.260
and a Cisco Container
Platform deployment, that

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.540
how are these
microservices or container

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:24.590
workloads communicating?

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:27.110
And what is the
latency between them?

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:29.420
And of course, security is
a very important aspect,

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:32.119
because as we see
workloads moving out

00:27:32.119 --> 00:27:34.160
of the on-prem environment,
outside the firewall,

00:27:34.160 --> 00:27:35.993
into the public cloud,
you want to make sure

00:27:35.993 --> 00:27:38.960
that there is security not just
at the infrastructure level,

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.040
but also from a
networking perspective.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.740
So you can create VPN tunnels.

00:27:42.740 --> 00:27:44.780
But at the HTTP
level, you need things

00:27:44.780 --> 00:27:47.240
like MTLS or mutual security--

00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.352
TLS security-- that ensures
that only the right services are

00:27:50.352 --> 00:27:52.310
talking to the other
services that are deployed

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:53.630
outside your environment.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:56.210
So Istio, with its
sidecar proxy model,

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.430
gives you all of
those capabilities

00:27:58.430 --> 00:27:59.500
in a hybrid environment.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:02.540
And as part of our use case
three, we provide that.

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:05.290
This is a very quick,
high-level architecture diagram

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:07.220
of how Istio is deployed.

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:09.840
You can see the control
plane has three components.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:12.797
It has the Istio Pilot, which
is responsible for essentially

00:28:12.797 --> 00:28:14.630
pushing down and doing
the service discovery

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:16.450
and pushing down
the routing rules.

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:20.780
Mixer is essentially
ingesting all the data

00:28:20.780 --> 00:28:23.819
from the sidecar to give you the
observability and the service

00:28:23.819 --> 00:28:26.360
graph and how communications
are happening in the [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:28.920
enforcement between
the different services.

00:28:28.920 --> 00:28:32.420
And finally, Citadel,
which is essentially

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:34.310
the service that's
providing MTLS

00:28:34.310 --> 00:28:37.790
between various services
that are running

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:39.810
as part of your service mesh.

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.920
So all of these components
talk to essentially Envoy,

00:28:42.920 --> 00:28:45.890
which is deployed within
each of your application

00:28:45.890 --> 00:28:47.230
or microservice.

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:50.600
And Envoy exposes API through
which it still communicates

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:52.250
and enforces the policies.

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:54.200
And that's what we'll
see in the demo.

00:28:54.200 --> 00:28:58.500
So specifically for
the hybrid use case

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:01.142
three, one of the features
that we have actually

00:29:01.142 --> 00:29:02.600
pushed into the
community, and it's

00:29:02.600 --> 00:29:05.180
going to be available
as part of Istio 1.0,

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:07.160
is the multi-cluster support.

00:29:07.160 --> 00:29:08.630
And my demo is all around this.

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:10.220
So it's very important
to understand

00:29:10.220 --> 00:29:12.440
what the multi-cluster
support is here.

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:14.240
So what multi-cluster
support does is,

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:17.000
you can have one single
Istio control plane.

00:29:17.000 --> 00:29:19.430
So you're having,
essentially, a single instance

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:21.920
of Pilot, Mixer, and Citadel.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.200
And that is essentially
pushing down policies

00:29:24.200 --> 00:29:27.160
into multiple Envoys or
services that are running

00:29:27.160 --> 00:29:28.810
in your hybrid environment.

00:29:28.810 --> 00:29:31.720
Now you may have seen
things like Traffic Director

00:29:31.720 --> 00:29:33.170
announcement
earlier from Google,

00:29:33.170 --> 00:29:34.870
which is a manage service.

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:38.050
With this kind of a model, you
can deploy an Istio control

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:40.630
plane either on Google
Kubernetes Engine,

00:29:40.630 --> 00:29:42.910
or you could deploy it on-prem.

00:29:42.910 --> 00:29:46.390
So it gives you the flexibility
to maintain and manage

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:49.180
your Kubernetes control plane,
as well as the Istio control

00:29:49.180 --> 00:29:51.550
plane, within your
own environment.

00:29:51.550 --> 00:29:53.560
And so that's a key piece.

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:55.039
So let's look at
the demo topology

00:29:55.039 --> 00:29:56.080
that we're going to show.

00:29:56.080 --> 00:29:58.690
This is the Booksinfo app--

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:00.790
again, available
on the Istio site.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:03.310
We've got a microservices
deployment made up

00:30:03.310 --> 00:30:05.920
of four different microservices,
our product page, which

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:07.340
is the main entry page.

00:30:07.340 --> 00:30:09.130
And it talks to the Reviews app.

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:10.600
It talks to Detail.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.580
And Reviews talks to Ratings.

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:15.220
So Reviews is essentially
getting Ratings information

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:16.534
from the Rating service.

00:30:16.534 --> 00:30:17.950
Now one thing to
note here is I've

00:30:17.950 --> 00:30:19.710
got three versions of Reviews.

00:30:19.710 --> 00:30:22.120
So Reviews 1 gives me no stars.

00:30:22.120 --> 00:30:23.620
Reviews 1 gives me black stars.

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:26.620
And Reviews 3
gives me red stars.

00:30:26.620 --> 00:30:28.630
So in our demo, what
we'd be doing essentially

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:32.770
is, we'll start with Reviews
v1 and v2 deployed on GKE.

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:36.700
And then we'll spin up
Reviews v3 on the on-prem side

00:30:36.700 --> 00:30:39.010
as a new service
and see how traffic

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:40.930
is getting load-balanced.

00:30:40.930 --> 00:30:43.170
So this is our
deployment architecture.

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:45.460
From the GKE side, we'll
have all the services

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:47.440
except Reviews v3 running.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:50.306
Again, this could also be a
completely flip deployment,

00:30:50.306 --> 00:30:52.680
where you could have all of
these things running on Cisco

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:54.040
Container Platform as well.

00:30:54.040 --> 00:30:55.780
But for this demo,
we'll have everything

00:30:55.780 --> 00:30:59.560
running, except for Reviews v3,
on Google Kubernetes engine.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:02.170
And then we'll spin
up Reviews v3 on CCP,

00:31:02.170 --> 00:31:05.662
or Cisco Container
Platform-based Kubernetes.

00:31:05.662 --> 00:31:07.120
One important thing
to note here is

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:10.040
the pod IP addresses have
completely different ranges.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.910
So how do you actually ensure
that you are discovering

00:31:12.910 --> 00:31:15.430
these services that are in
completely different subnets

00:31:15.430 --> 00:31:17.025
across these two environments?

00:31:17.025 --> 00:31:18.400
So that's where
some of the magic

00:31:18.400 --> 00:31:20.066
that we have done,
in terms of enabling,

00:31:20.066 --> 00:31:22.090
for example, the
Cloud Services router

00:31:22.090 --> 00:31:24.160
