We'll give it a couple of
seconds for it to come up.

00:36:08.310 --> 00:36:11.160
As it comes up, what is
happening in the background

00:36:11.160 --> 00:36:14.577
is essentially this service,
with the sidecar proxy

00:36:14.577 --> 00:36:16.950
is getting registered
with my Istio control

00:36:16.950 --> 00:36:19.590
plane that is running on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:23.400
And as that gets registered,
the control plane essentially

00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.400
gets to know that if
there is a request that

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:28.770
is going to Reviews, I can send
it across to a new version that

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.070
has just come up.

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:32.130
So let's go back
to our app here.

00:36:32.130 --> 00:36:34.500
And I'm going to start
refreshing here again.

00:36:34.500 --> 00:36:38.290
And what are you seeing
are red stars come up.

00:36:38.290 --> 00:36:41.257
And essentially, this tells
me that with black stars,

00:36:41.257 --> 00:36:42.840
that I am now starting
to load balance

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:45.300
across multiple versions
of my Reviews app

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:49.410
that was running across on-prem,
as well as GKE environment.

00:36:49.410 --> 00:36:51.660
So again, you can see I'm
continuously just refreshing

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:52.210
it.

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:53.918
And I'm seeing black
stars and red stars.

00:36:53.918 --> 00:36:55.709
And there should be
no stars as well.

00:36:55.709 --> 00:36:56.250
There you go.

00:36:56.250 --> 00:36:59.910
So in a random load balancing
fashion, this is happening.

00:36:59.910 --> 00:37:01.410
Traditionally, or
previously, if you

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:04.350
had to do this in
your environment,

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:06.720
across a public cloud and
an on-prem environment,

00:37:06.720 --> 00:37:09.990
you would have probably
a load balancer.

00:37:09.990 --> 00:37:12.570
And that load balancer,
you would have a VIP.

00:37:12.570 --> 00:37:14.580
And that VIP
somehow needs to get

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:17.160
configured with a new back
end of your service version

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:17.882
that comes up.

00:37:17.882 --> 00:37:20.340
And there would have been new
manual configuration that you

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:22.798
would have to push in into
the load balance [INAUDIBLE]----

00:37:22.798 --> 00:37:23.560
all of that.

00:37:23.560 --> 00:37:26.580
But as you can see in this
demo, as your devleopers spun up

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:30.405
a new version of an application,
your operations team,

00:37:30.405 --> 00:37:32.820
with this Istio
control plane, can now

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:36.150
actually observe that a
new version has come up

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:38.610
and assign rules, in
order to send the traffic

00:37:38.610 --> 00:37:40.860
to these new versions
in a very dynamic

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:42.930
and a very consistent way.

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:44.550
So that's one
aspect of the demo.

00:37:44.550 --> 00:37:48.270
Now let's say your
developer says,

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:52.020
Reviews 3 is actually the best
version that I've seen so far,

00:37:52.020 --> 00:37:53.850
and I want all of
my client traffic

00:37:53.850 --> 00:37:56.010
that's coming from
anywhere in the world

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:58.800
to be now sent to Reviews v3.

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:02.490
So how do I actually apply this
policy into my service mesh

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:05.190
without disrupting the
traffic to all other versions,

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:08.450
and maybe without even taking
down my Reviews v1 and v2?

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:10.470
So before that, what
I'm going to do is I'm

00:38:10.470 --> 00:38:12.900
going to switch back
into my GKE environment,

00:38:12.900 --> 00:38:15.610
because that's where my Istio
control plane is running.

00:38:15.610 --> 00:38:18.450
And I'm going to look
into a definition here

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:22.530
of a policy which is a virtual
service construct in Istio.

00:38:22.530 --> 00:38:24.720
And this essentially
is a policy definition

00:38:24.720 --> 00:38:28.490
that says that if any traffic
to a destination called Reviews,

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:30.810
I need to send it to v3.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:32.550
So Istio understands this.

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:34.440
It can push down into
the Envoy sidecar

00:38:34.440 --> 00:38:36.540
proxy that's running
in my service mesh

00:38:36.540 --> 00:38:38.100
across these two environments.

00:38:38.100 --> 00:38:41.310
And then hopefully, as we try to
again refresh our product page

00:38:41.310 --> 00:38:44.430
entry, it will send all
the traffic to Reviews v3.

00:38:44.430 --> 00:38:47.310
So let's go ahead and do
that again with a kubectl

00:38:47.310 --> 00:38:51.340
create command here
and clear that policy.

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:53.310
So I just created that
virtual service policy.

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:57.300
And it's implementing that into
all the Envoy sidecars that are

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:58.800
running within my deployment.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:00.960
And I'm going to start
refreshing here again.

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:03.850
And what do you see here
now as I refresh this?

00:39:03.850 --> 00:39:05.520
Only red stars
are getting shown.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:07.020
I'm not seeing any black stars.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:10.350
I'm not seeing any no stars,
which essentially is telling me

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:13.470
that that policy just got
applied into my service mesh,

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:16.320
and my developer gave
thumbs up to this version,

00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:18.420
and my operator is
now able to monitor

00:39:18.420 --> 00:39:20.760
how my service is
getting communicated

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.054
between the different
microservices.

00:39:23.054 --> 00:39:26.571
Additionally, you can
see here a full graph

00:39:26.571 --> 00:39:29.070
of all the communication that
is happening within my service

00:39:29.070 --> 00:39:30.390
deployment.

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:33.270
So Envoy, from an
observability point of view,

00:39:33.270 --> 00:39:35.091
gives you this right
out of the box--

00:39:35.091 --> 00:39:36.840
that these are the
different services that

00:39:36.840 --> 00:39:39.256
are part of the service mesh
and the number of connections

00:39:39.256 --> 00:39:42.060
and requests that are going
between these services.

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:44.250
Now let's say that
you actually wanted

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:47.220
to go back and do, again,
the random load balancing,

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:50.190
because you started to see
some service degradation.

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:51.960
In that case, you'd
essentially want

00:39:51.960 --> 00:39:55.360
to go back here and
just delete this policy.

00:39:55.360 --> 00:39:58.020
And hopefully, now
everything should come back

00:39:58.020 --> 00:40:00.480
to what it was before, which
is the load balance across all

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:02.130
the three versions of Reviews.

00:40:02.130 --> 00:40:05.515
So if we go back here and
start refreshing our app here,

00:40:05.515 --> 00:40:06.890
and you can see
black stars start

00:40:06.890 --> 00:40:09.030
to pop up, red stars, no stars.

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:12.450
So I'm able to roll back my
policy definitions, based

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:15.600
on how my services are
essentially performing

00:40:15.600 --> 00:40:19.224
into my deployment, and
make those decisions quickly

00:40:19.224 --> 00:40:21.390
and implement them very
quickly in a very consistent

00:40:21.390 --> 00:40:23.820
environment across a
hybrid environment,

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:26.850
because typically, you would
do these things in a cloud

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:29.550
environment, using things like
security groups or firewalls

00:40:29.550 --> 00:40:30.750
within those environments.

00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:32.125
And in the on
prem-side, you will

00:40:32.125 --> 00:40:33.920
have, again, similar devices.

00:40:33.920 --> 00:40:37.050
But with the Istio and
service mesh model,

00:40:37.050 --> 00:40:40.370
you essentially have a layer
between your LTL4 and L7

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:44.040
to apply these policies once
and apply them very consistently

00:40:44.040 --> 00:40:47.040
across these multiple
environments.

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:49.970
So let's switch back
here to our slides.

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:52.050
That was essentially the demo.

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.080
So like I mentioned,
in this set-up,

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:56.720
there was networking also
going on in the back end,

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:59.450
from an LTL4 perspective,
as well as security.

00:40:59.450 --> 00:41:02.000
And that is essentially cutting
across all of our three use

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:03.480
cases that we have.

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:05.990
So first and foremost was the
Stealthwatch Cloud integration

00:41:05.990 --> 00:41:11.372
with Google Cloud or
Google VPC, specifically.

00:41:11.372 --> 00:41:12.830
Essentially, our
Stealthwatch Cloud

00:41:12.830 --> 00:41:15.620
is essentially a SaaS
product from Cisco

00:41:15.620 --> 00:41:18.500
that enables you to monitor
the network connections going

00:41:18.500 --> 00:41:20.630
into your VPC resources
in a cloud environment

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:23.060
and also your on-prem
review resources.

00:41:23.060 --> 00:41:24.650
Here we have
integrated specifically

00:41:24.650 --> 00:41:27.080
with VPC flow logs
in Google Cloud.

00:41:27.080 --> 00:41:30.650
So you can ingest all the VPC
flow logs on the Google Cloud

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:32.810
into the Stealthwatch
Cloud service

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:34.430
to give you visibility
of things like,

00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:36.430
what are the number of
network connections going

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:40.070
into your Kubernetes pods,
or to your virtual machines?

00:41:40.070 --> 00:41:42.650
Similarly, on the on-prem
environment in Cisco Container

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:44.180
platform, we enable
the deployment

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:47.960
of Stealthwatch sensors, which
essentially puts all NetFlow

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:50.180
data into the SaaS
service that's

00:41:50.180 --> 00:41:51.830
provided by Stealthwatch Cloud.

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:53.870
This service, again,
was available--

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:56.220
has been GA-ed in
March earlier this year

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:58.670
and is essentially
part of the solution.

00:41:58.670 --> 00:42:00.170
The second integration
that was also

00:42:00.170 --> 00:42:02.330
in the demo, running
in the background,

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:04.310
was Cloud Services
Router 1000v--

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:07.550
Cisco Cloud Services Router
1000v-- on Google Cloud.

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:11.060
Our CSR 1000v has been very
popular in other clouds.

00:42:11.060 --> 00:42:13.202
And based on a lot of
the customer requests,

00:42:13.202 --> 00:42:14.660
and as part of this
partnership, we

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:17.630
wanted to give a very
similar iOS on-prem

00:42:17.630 --> 00:42:21.180
experience on Google
Cloud for our customers.

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:23.240
And that is what
CSR 1000v provides.

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:25.340
It gives you extended
capabilities,

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:28.334
not just from an IPSec
perspective, but also DMVPN

00:42:28.334 --> 00:42:29.750
and [INAUDIBLE]
that you can apply

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:34.100
across multiple on-prem data
centers or campus and branches,

00:42:34.100 --> 00:42:36.844
using the CSR running
on Google Cloud.

00:42:36.844 --> 00:42:38.510
Again, this will be
part of the solution

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:41.100
and enables you to give that
pod-to-pod connectivity in case

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:43.820
if they're running
in different subnets.

00:42:43.820 --> 00:42:45.830
So let's bring all
of this together.

00:42:45.830 --> 00:42:48.530
So we've talked a lot
about three different use

00:42:48.530 --> 00:42:50.090
cases, different components.

00:42:50.090 --> 00:42:51.917
So how does all of
this come together?

00:42:51.917 --> 00:42:53.500
So as you can see
on the on-prem side,

00:42:53.500 --> 00:42:55.570
it's starting with
the infrastructure.

00:42:55.570 --> 00:42:56.570
We have got HyperFlex.

00:42:56.570 --> 00:42:58.354
We have got ACI from Cisco.

00:42:58.354 --> 00:43:00.020
We have got the Cisco
Container Platform

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:01.870
that integrates with these.

00:43:01.870 --> 00:43:04.580
We've got Service Catalog
that enables you to consume,

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:07.570
via Open Service Broker, all
the Google Cloud Services.

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:09.350
We've got Istio that
is running on-prem

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:12.980
or in Google Cloud to give
you the service mesh layer.

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:15.650
And then Apigee gives you,
on-prem, the capability

00:43:15.650 --> 00:43:18.260
to expose your traditional
systems for things

00:43:18.260 --> 00:43:20.450
that are running in
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:43:20.450 --> 00:43:23.030
And then finally, Cloud Center
that gives you the capability

00:43:23.030 --> 00:43:26.250
to deploy these things
across multiple environments.

00:43:26.250 --> 00:43:28.100
So this is our overall
architecture that

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:30.310
brings everything together.

00:43:30.310 --> 00:43:32.150
This is going to be,
as Allan mentioned,

00:43:32.150 --> 00:43:33.830
a Cisco Validated Design.

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:36.290
So all the components that
you see in this picture,

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:38.510
they will be available to
you as single products.

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:40.390
But with the joint
partnership, we

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:42.560
are going to be giving you
a validated design that

00:43:42.560 --> 00:43:45.140
ensures that these are
working together to give you

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:48.500
the benefit of making them work
together, as well as supported

00:43:48.500 --> 00:43:50.310
jointly by Cisco and Google.

00:43:50.310 --> 00:43:52.420
So things like, when you
need Kubernetes support,

00:43:52.420 --> 00:43:53.900
when you need
Istio support, they

00:43:53.900 --> 00:43:55.850
will be jointly supported
by Cisco and Google

00:43:55.850 --> 00:43:58.370
as part of this entire
partnership and solution.

00:43:58.370 --> 00:44:02.000
Now one of the things
that we have done here

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:05.570
over the last few months as we
have announced this partnership

00:44:05.570 --> 00:44:08.180
are the various milestones
that you see in this picture.

00:44:08.180 --> 00:44:12.200
So Cisco joined the Istio
community back in July 2017.

00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:14.050
The feature that I
just showed you was,

00:44:14.050 --> 00:44:16.280
well, pushed through
Cisco, working very closely

00:44:16.280 --> 00:44:17.780
with Google engineers.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:21.110
We announced the hybrid cloud
partnership back in October

00:44:21.110 --> 00:44:23.594
at the Cisco's Partner Summit.

00:44:23.594 --> 00:44:25.760
In November, we announced
the multi-cloud portfolio,

00:44:25.760 --> 00:44:28.430
in which hybrid cloud
is also part of it.

00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:29.990
January, in Cisco
Live Barcelona,

00:44:29.990 --> 00:44:32.390
we announced the Cisco
Container Platform, and then

00:44:32.390 --> 00:44:34.820
Stealthwatch Cloud and the
CloudCenter app [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:34.820 --> 00:44:36.530
Kubernetes support followed on.

00:44:36.530 --> 00:44:38.810
At this conference, we
announced another thing,

00:44:38.810 --> 00:44:42.180
was the Cisco and Google
Cloud developer challenge.

00:44:42.180 --> 00:44:44.360
So this is a challenge
that essentially leverages

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:45.980
a lot of the
technology components

00:44:45.980 --> 00:44:48.000
that we have talked
about so far--

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:50.570
so things like Cisco Container
Platform, Google Kubernetes

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:52.370
Engine, Istio.

00:44:52.370 --> 00:44:54.140
And there are
multiple prizes that

00:44:54.140 --> 00:44:56.000
are to be awarded as
part of this challenge.

00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:58.110
We have opened up
registration for it.

00:44:58.110 --> 00:45:00.780
Here's the URL for it
that you can check it out.

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:02.840
But the key thing
to remember here

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:07.160
is that we see not only those
three use cases as the starting

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:08.990
point, but opening
up the technology

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:12.560
components to our customers,
to our developers and partners

00:45:12.560 --> 00:45:16.010
to innovate around
using these use case.

00:45:16.010 --> 00:45:19.010
So at the end, closing this--
and we'll open up for questions

00:45:19.010 --> 00:45:19.550
here--

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:21.260
is that we want to
essentially give you

00:45:21.260 --> 00:45:23.960
a platform that is
supported by Cisco

00:45:23.960 --> 00:45:26.910
that enables you to move to the
public cloud at your own pace.

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:29.240
You can innovate and
move to modernization,

00:45:29.240 --> 00:45:32.030
as well as leverage
open source components

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.430
in your environment that
is supported jointly

00:45:34.430 --> 00:45:35.540
by Cisco and Google.

00:45:35.540 --> 00:45:37.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

